<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Freedmen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Freedmen were former slaves who had been granted their freedom. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> they formed a significant social group; they gained citizenship but faced some restrictions, and some became wealthy or held influential posts in the imperial household.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Buyid armies combined two very different forces: Daylamite infantry, the dynasty's own countrymen, fighting with spears, javelins and large shields, and Turkish cavalry, mostly slave soldiers or <b>freedmen</b>, who provided the mobility the Daylamites lacked.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nation’s history also includes slavery: the Cherokee <b>Freedmen</b> were promised the rights of native Cherokees by the treaty of 1866, a right confirmed by a federal court in 2017.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cherokee-nation">Cherokee Nation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman society was rigidly stratified between the senatorial order, the equestrian order, the plebeian citizenry, <b>freedmen</b> and the enslaved, who at the empire's height are estimated to have made up perhaps a quarter or more of the population of Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Soldiers were strongly represented, with merchants, customs officials, minor bureaucrats and many <b>freedmen</b> and slaves; few came from aristocratic or senatorial families before the pagan revival of the mid-4th century.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>A household of slaves and <b>freedmen</b> ran much of his government, and his portrait was on the coinage.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-rome">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Rome</a></li><li><blockquote>…abolitionist and Harvard graduate who came to supervise the island's plantations from 1862 to 1865, wrote it down in music notation as he heard the <b>freedmen</b> sing it, and his cousin William Francis Allen reported in 1863 that they sang it as they rowed him across Station Creek.</blockquote><a href="/songs/michael-row-the-boat-ashore">Michael, Row the Boat Ashore</a></li></ul></section>
